[Music]  looking live there on Capitol Hill we  have very special guests to bring in  Daniel Krauthammer he is the son of  longtime syndicated columnist and Fox  News contributor Charles Krauthammer he  is a friend as his father was and he has  compiled a book of his late father's  work finished and completed his late  father's work books called the point of  it all  a lifetime of great loves and endeavors  which comes out tomorrow and Daniel  thank you for doing this thank you for  having me on fact that you chose special  rapport in this very chair to be the  first interview is important to us it's  important to me it's it's the right  place to do it the only place to do it I  think how did this come to be  so my father was working on a second  collection a follow-up to his 2013 book  things that matter and he was a long way  into the process when his health crisis  struck a little over a year and ago now  I guess 16 months ago now and he was  able to continue working on it  a little bit during his hospitalization  which I helped him with and we kind of  jerry-rigged as his room to help him use  the computer and be able to look at this  and continue compiling it but when the  final diagnosis came he entrusted me to  finish it for him and so since he passed  in June that's been my absolute number  one priority and what was it that he  said to you about compiling this book  and I should point out you do the  foreword and also a beautiful ending  which actually is the eulogy you  delivered at his funeral yeah I had a  sense of where he wanted to go with it  he had already chosen most of what's in  the book now there was still quite a bit  to be added and and to be arranged and  make sure that it all really fit  together and that the core ideas spoke  through which wasn't too hard because  his thinking and his clarity were so  apparent and they  coming out from reading the book front  to back but I I finished that put it  together and as you said I wrote an  introduction to explain how I thought it  all came together in conjunction with  his politics his personality his  philosophy and his fun passions and the  humor and as you say the last entry in  the book is the eulogy I delivered at  his funeral which was a difficult  decision to put that in because it's  obviously very personal and close to my  heart but I I wanted to the world to  know how wonderful he was and how  extraordinary he was and that was the  best way I knew how  and I interviewed him many times for the  hour that we did in subsequent hours and  I tried to get him to talk about you and  your mom Robin and he was always a  little hesitant to do that a lot  hesitant I should say this book has a  lot more personal information that I had  ever heard Charles talk about yeah and  that was that wasn't an easy decision  either because as you say he was very  private especially about me and my  mother and I think I love him for that  and he was protecting us and not wanting  to make us part of his profession his  career and I thought long and deep about  how much and how personal to get in this  book but as I wrote an introduction now  that he is gone it's different and I I  think there's a power to his voice his  first person voice speaking as himself  and not just his political arguments  that so many people have reached out to  me and spoken to me about in the months  since and I realized how much he really  touched a lot of people's lives and I I  wanted the world to be able to see that  and read that and I I think he would  approve I'm sure he would we miss his  voice every night on this panel I can't  tell you how many emails and tweets and  messages I gets and I wonder what  Charles would think about  this I really miss his voice you know  today with the remembrance of the 41st  president found this picture with your  dad and your mom with President George  HW Bush and a reflection of a column  looking back after he loses in 1992 in  which he says quote bushes was not a  failed presidency but a completed one  history called him to do two things to  close out the Cold War and thwart the  ambitions of a reckless tyrant in the  Persian Gulf Bush did both rather well  Bush was born and trained for these two  jobs but only these two by the end of  four years there was nothing left for  him to do it is also the reason his  country turned him out of office the  voters judgment however is not to be  confused with history's it may suit  partisan journalism to equate losing  with failure but history does not follow  the polls what's amazing about Charles  is he could always cut through the noise  now looking back everyone is saying this  saying this moment this presidency was  larger than the 38% he received in that  election but he said it back in 1992 I  know I had the same feeling actually I  was looking back at his old columns when  I heard the news about former President  Bush and I was struck by this too that I  looked at the Dateline and as with  actually so many pieces of his writing  that are in his book and other places it  feels like it could be written today and  I remember you were talking about just  earlier on the show but the mention of  Churchill and Truman which are my dad  also mentioned in this article that you  know he had a very deep sense of history  which comes through in his writing and  his sense that some of the greatest  leaders are they accomplished what they  need to for history and then their turn  is over and it was a very poetic  appreciation of history I thought  there's amazing things in this book  about foreign policy about domestic  policy longer kind of than his columns  even their speeches stuff that nuggets  that you really wouldn't even see in  writings ya know there's quite a few  pieces in this book that haven't been  seen or published anywhere before as you  say some speeches one completely new  original long essay about the rise of  authoritarianism and populism that he  wrote in July 2017 that hadn't been  published and some long ruminations on  the big ideas of what's America's role  in the world what are the ideological  challenges to democracy in the 21st  century along with fun stuff like  baseball and chess and all the rest of  it he was such an interesting character  but I know most people saying looking at  this interview will say just ask what  was it like having Charles as your dad  wonderful  I think asked about his limitations and  obviously he had limitations but to you  you didn't see him no I think you know  you know what you know when you grow up  and I think he faced difficulties in his  life and everyone faces difficulties in  their life and he lives such a full and  vibrant life in the life he wanted and  he made it the life that he wanted for  me too  and I never felt wanting for anything  and in fact as I've gotten older I've  only been able to look back and realize  just how lucky I was and especially now  I think about that all the time well we  were lucky to have him  for the years that we did especially  here on special report this book I am  honored to give a blurb on the back it  says this there are people who might  meet you meet throughout your life that  you just know will leave an impact  forever Charles Krauthammer was one of  those people his words and his knowledge  and his eloquence on the issues of the  day were unsurpassed he could cut  through the noise he could get to the  point better than anybody it's a real  honor I'm gonna sell a hundred these 100  million of these books if I can  thank you very much thank you breath  good luck thank

